1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,413
This is CoCo Token Meter.

2
00:00:02,463 --> 00:00:08,385
It shows what every Snowflake CoCo chat costs, in tokens and credits.

3
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:11,660
Installing it starts on GitHub.

4
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:17,026
Open the file called install in Snowsight, and copy it with one click.

5
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:21,308
In Snowsight, paste it into a new SQL file.

6
00:00:21,358 --> 00:00:25,651
Then choose the account admin role, and a warehouse.

7
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:27,845
Now choose Run All.

8
00:00:27,895 --> 00:00:33,903
The script creates the usage views, the admin dashboard, and the CoCo skills.

9
00:00:33,953 --> 00:00:39,724
It runs 62 statements, and on this account that took about thirty seconds.

10
00:00:40,467 --> 00:00:42,919
The last result confirms it.

11
00:00:42,969 --> 00:00:45,600
CoCo Token Meter is installed.

12
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:50,098
Now for the question CoCo could not answer before.

13
00:00:50,148 --> 00:00:52,081
Token usage for this chat.

14
00:00:52,131 --> 00:00:58,184
CoCo loads the skill, finds the ID of the open chat, and queries the usage view.

15
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:00,541
And here is the answer.

16
00:01:00,591 --> 00:01:07,819
Seven requests, seven hundred and twenty two thousand tokens, and about zero point three two credits.

17
00:01:07,869 --> 00:01:10,629
The first request is marked cold start.

18
00:01:10,679 --> 00:01:16,682
That is the chat loading its starting context, and it is more than half of the cost.

19
00:01:17,432 --> 00:01:17,846
Next.

20
00:01:17,896 --> 00:01:20,996
Show my CoCo token usage per chat.

21
00:01:21,046 --> 00:01:25,629
CoCo first asks permission to fetch my chat names.

22
00:01:26,366 --> 00:01:32,905
Then it lists my recent chats by name, with requests, tokens, and credits for each one.

23
00:01:32,955 --> 00:01:38,357
This chat is the most expensive so far, at about zero point six credits.

24
00:01:39,099 --> 00:01:41,446
Admins can ask about everyone.

25
00:01:41,496 --> 00:01:43,443
CoCo usage for all users.

26
00:01:43,493 --> 00:01:52,710
Five users, eighty five chats, and nineteen point four seven credits in total, with each user's most expensive chat.

27
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:59,093
The same data drives an admin dashboard, built with Streamlit in Snowflake.

28
00:01:59,143 --> 00:02:05,912
The overview shows total credits, the number of chats, and the share spent on cold starts.

29
00:02:05,962 --> 00:02:08,488
Here, that is seventy one percent.

30
00:02:09,227 --> 00:02:15,450
Below are credits per day, where the credits went, and a heatmap of when CoCo is used.

31
00:02:16,193 --> 00:02:20,142
The Chats view ranks the most expensive chats by name.

32
00:02:20,192 --> 00:02:25,028
The Users view draws every chat as a block, sized by what it cost.

33
00:02:25,778 --> 00:02:34,436
Savings estimates what could have been avoided, by asking follow-ups instead of starting new chats, and by avoiding long pauses.

34
00:02:35,178 --> 00:02:39,727
And the chat explorer shows a single chat, request by request.

35
00:02:39,777 --> 00:02:42,545
The tall yellow bars are idle reloads.

36
00:02:42,595 --> 00:02:47,367
I came back after a break, and paid to load the whole chat again.

37
00:02:48,112 --> 00:02:49,840
Three things I learned.

38
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:55,328
A new chat costs about zero point two credits, before it does any work.

39
00:02:55,378 --> 00:03:00,198
Around seventy percent of my credits went to those cold starts.

40
00:03:00,248 --> 00:03:02,439
And the cheapest fix is free.

41
00:03:02,489 --> 00:03:04,526
Ask follow-ups in one chat.

42
00:03:05,477 --> 00:03:08,392
CoCo Token Meter is open source.

43
00:03:08,442 --> 00:03:10,430
The link is on screen.

